## Introduction & Architecture

### What is Tableau?

```
Tableau: Business intelligence and data visualization platform

Core Purpose:
Transform raw data → Interactive visualizations → Insights

Components:

1. Tableau Desktop:
   - Author worksheets and dashboards
   - Connect to data sources
   - Create calculated fields
   - Publish to server/cloud

2. Tableau Server/Online:
   - Host and share dashboards
   - Collaboration
   - Schedule refreshes
   - User permissions

3. Tableau Public:
   - Free, cloud-hosted
   - Public visualizations
   - Community sharing
   - No private data
```

### Why Tableau?

```
Problems it solves:
✓ Data exploration: Find patterns quickly
✓ Communication: Visualize insights clearly
✓ Speed: Minutes to interactive dashboard (not hours coding)
✓ Flexibility: Drag-drop interface (no coding)
✓ Scalability: Handles millions of rows efficiently

Comparison:

Tableau:
+ Visual, intuitive interface
+ Fast dashboard creation
+ Beautiful defaults
+ Strong interactivity
- Expensive licensing
- Less code flexibility
- Steep learning curve initially

Python (Matplotlib/Seaborn/Plotly):
+ Open source, free
+ Complete control
+ Code version control
- Slower to create
- Requires programming
+ Reproducible analysis

Business Intelligence Tools:
- Power BI: Microsoft ecosystem, cheaper
- Qlik: Associative engine
- Google Data Studio: Free, limited
```

### Architecture Overview

```
Data Flow in Tableau:

Data Sources
├─ Relational DB (SQL Server, PostgreSQL)
├─ Cloud (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift)
├─ Files (Excel, CSV, JSON)
└─ APIs (Salesforce, Google Analytics)
    ↓
Tableau Desktop
├─ Data source (connection settings)
├─ Worksheets (individual visualizations)
├─ Dashboards (multiple sheets combined)
└─ Stories (narrative progression)
    ↓
Publishing
├─ Tableau Server (on-premise)
├─ Tableau Online (SaaS)
└─ Tableau Public (free, public)
    ↓
Consumption
├─ Web browser
├─ Tableau Mobile app
└─ Embedded views
```

