## Theory & Visualization Principles

### Information Hierarchy

```
Visual Hierarchy Principle:
Most important information appears first, most prominent

Implementation:

1. Spatial Position (Reading Order):
   Top-left → Top-right → Bottom-left → Bottom-right
   
   Place most critical metric top-left
   Place supporting details bottom-right

2. Size:
   Larger = more important
   
   Hero visualization: 40% of dashboard
   Supporting visuals: 20% each
   Details: 10-15%

3. Color Saturation:
   Bright colors = important
   Muted/grey = context
   
   Red: Alert/negative
   Green: Success/positive
   Blue/grey: Neutral context

4. Placement Principle:
   │ MOST IMPORTANT   │ IMPORTANT     │
   │ (Hero viz)       │ (Supporting)  │
   ├──────────────────┼───────────────┤
   │ SUPPORTING       │ DETAIL/LESS   │
   │ (Filters, info)  │ IMPORTANT     │
```

### Color Theory for Data

```
Sequential Color Scheme:
Values from low to high
Color progression: Light → Dark (one hue)
Example: White → Blue → Dark Blue

Use when:
✓ Single measure (profit, sales, temperature)
✓ No zero/neutral point
✓ Want to show magnitude

Diverging Color Scheme:
Opposite ends are different colors
Neutral in middle
Example: Red ← White → Blue

Use when:
✓ Comparing to baseline (variance)
✓ Positive vs negative values
✓ Above vs below average

Categorical Color Scheme:
Different hues for categories
Each color distinct
Example: Red, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange

Use when:
✓ No order (product categories, regions)
✓ Maximum 5-7 categories
✓ Each category equally important

Color Psychology:
- Red: Warning, loss, negative
- Green: Success, gain, positive
- Blue: Trust, neutral, informational
- Orange: Attention, highlight
- Grey: Context, disabled, neutral

Accessibility:
✓ 8% of males colorblind (red-green)
✓ Avoid red-green only (use shape too)
✓ High contrast (dark on light)
✓ Avoid pure red/blue combinations
```

### Chart Type Selection Logic

```
Question: What relationship am I exploring?

                        ┌─ Comparison (A vs B)?
                        │  ├─ Same time: Bar chart
                        │  ├─ Different items: Bullet chart
                        │  └─ Many items: Small multiples
                        │
            ┌─ Univariate─┤
            │             └─ Distribution?
            │                ├─ Histogram (continuous)
            │                ├─ Box plot (quartiles + outliers)
            │                └─ Density plot (shape)
            │
Question───┤            ┌─ Correlation (X vs Y)?
            │    ├─ Scatter plot
            │    └─ Size/color: Third variable
            │
            ├─ Multivariate─┤
            │                └─ Composition (parts of whole)?
            │                   ├─ Pie chart (≤5 slices)
            │                   ├─ Stacked bar (compare totals + parts)
            │                   └─ Treemap (hierarchical)
            │
            └─ Temporal─────┐
                            ├─ Trend (X = time)?
                            │  ├─ Line chart (continuous)
                            │  └─ Area chart (magnitude)
                            │
                            └─ Sequence?
                               ├─ Gantt (project timeline)
                               └─ Sankey (flow through stages)

Pattern Recognition (Why people read charts):
1. Comparison: "How much bigger?"
2. Part-to-whole: "What percentage?"
3. Trend: "Is it increasing?"
4. Outliers: "What doesn't fit?"
5. Correlation: "How related are X and Y?"

Design Decisions:
- Pre-attentive attributes detected in <500ms:
  ✓ Position (most accurate)
  ✓ Color (strong)
  ✓ Size (medium)
  ✗ Angle (weak)
  ✗ Shape (weak)
  
- Use position for most important comparison
- Use color for categories/status
- Use size for magnitude
```

### Gestalt Principles in Dashboard Design

```
Proximity:
Objects close together appear related

Example:
┌────────────────────────────┐
│  KPI Cards (grouped close) │
├────────────────────────────┤
│  (space)                   │
├────────────────────────────┤
│  Visualizations (grouped)  │
└────────────────────────────┘

Users group KPI cards together (they're close)
Mentally separate from below (space between)

Similarity:
Objects that look alike are perceived as related

Example:
All regional sales cards same color → Regional family
All product cards different color → Different category

Continuity:
Elements in line appear related

Good: ──── (line guides eye)
Bad:  ╱╱  ╲╲ (disjointed, confusing)

Put related metrics in rows/columns

Enclosure:
Elements in container appear grouped

Example:
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ KPI SECTION                 │
│ ├─ Total Sales  ├─ Growth % │
│ ├─ Orders       ├─ Target % │
└─────────────────────────────┘

Closure:
Humans complete incomplete shapes

Use borders sparingly (implies grouping)
Whitespace can suggest grouping

Applying to Dashboard:
1. Group related metrics (proximity)
2. Use same styling (similarity)
3. Arrange in order of importance
4. Add white space (separation)
5. Use containers for sections (enclosure)
```

### Data Density vs Cognitive Load

```
Data Density: How much information per square inch

High Density:
├─ Show more detail
├─ Smaller size
├─ Complex relationships
└─ For: Experts, exploratory analysis

Low Density:
├─ Show one idea
├─ Larger size
├─ Simple relationships
└─ For: Executives, specific question

Balance:

Tableau default is good for ~100 rows
More than that?
├─ Use filters (reduce scope)
├─ Aggregate higher level
├─ Show top/bottom N
└─ Use small multiples

Too much info in one view?
├─ Split into two sheets
├─ Use tooltips for detail
├─ Progressive disclosure (filters)
└─ Drill-down capability

Cognitive Load:

Readers process charts in stages:
1. Encoding (what data is shown?)
2. Understanding (what does it mean?)
3. Decision (what action?)

High cognitive load → Skip step 3 (no action)

Reduce load by:
✓ Clear title (what is this?)
✓ Axis labels (what are dimensions?)
✓ Color legend (what does color mean?)
✓ Data labels (exact values)
✓ Reference lines (context)
✗ Excessive decoration
✗ 3D effects
✗ Unexpected colors
✗ Multiple chart types in one view
```

