## Core Concepts & Fundamentals

### Shelves: Rows, Columns, Marks

```
Every Tableau chart has:

ROWS shelf: What goes down the chart
COLUMNS shelf: What goes across the chart
MARKS shelf: Visual encoding (color, size, shape)

Example: Sales by Region and Quarter

Columns shelf: [Quarter]  → X-axis labels
Rows shelf: [Region]     → Y-axis labels
Marks: SUM([Sales])      → Values in cells

Result:
        Q1        Q2        Q3        Q4
West   100k      150k      180k      200k
East   120k      140k      160k      190k
South   80k      100k      120k      140k
```

### Dimensions vs Measures

```
DIMENSIONS: Categorical, "what" and "who"
- Discrete values
- Appear in blue (in Data pane)
- Create groups, filters
- Examples: Region, Product, Date, Customer Name

MEASURES: Numerical, "how much"
- Continuous values
- Appear in green (in Data pane)
- Aggregated (SUM, AVG, COUNT)
- Examples: Sales, Profit, Quantity

Tableau Inference:
- Dimensions by default: Text fields, dates
- Measures by default: Numbers

Can override:
- Treat dimension as measure: SUM(ID)
- Treat measure as dimension: Region (if used as filter)
```

### Aggregation Levels

```
Aggregation: Combining multiple values

Common Aggregations:
- SUM: Total (Sales)
- AVG: Average (Profit Margin)
- COUNT: Number of records
- COUNT(DISTINCT): Unique values
- MIN/MAX: Smallest/largest
- MEDIAN: Middle value
- PERCENTILE: Position in distribution
- STDEV: Spread of data

Context matters:
Dimensions in view → Measure aggregated to that level

Example:
[Region] in rows → [Sales] summed by region
[Region] + [Product] in rows → [Sales] summed by region × product
```

