## Complete Concepts & Keypoints Reference

### Dimensions (What/Who/Where/When)

```
Characteristics:
✓ Categorical (discrete) values
✓ Create groups and filters
✓ Appear in blue in data pane
✓ Define granularity of analysis

Examples:
- Temporal: Date, Month, Year, Day
- Geographic: Region, Country, State, City
- Product: Category, Subcategory, Brand
- Customer: Name, Segment, Industry
- Organization: Department, Team, Manager

In Dashboard:
- Use in rows/columns to group data
- Use in filters to narrow focus
- Dragging dimension → Creates new visualization
- Multiple dimensions → Cross-tabulation (matrix)
```

### Measures (How Much)

```
Characteristics:
✓ Numeric values
✓ Require aggregation (SUM, AVG, COUNT)
✓ Appear in green in data pane
✓ Usually axis values

Common Aggregations:
- SUM: Total (most common)
- AVG: Average (average order value)
- COUNT: Number of records
- COUNT(DISTINCT): Unique customers
- MIN/MAX: Extreme values
- MEDIAN: Middle value
- PERCENTILE: Position (50th percentile = median)
- STDEV: Spread/variability

In Dashboard:
- Measure on axis shows magnitude
- Multiple measures → Dual axis charts
- Color by measure → Heat maps
- Size by measure → Bubble charts
```

### Calculated Fields (Custom Metrics)

```
Purpose: Create metrics from raw fields

Types:

1. Arithmetic:
   [Sales] - [Cost] = [Profit]
   [Profit] / [Sales] = [Profit Margin]

2. Conditional:
   IF [Sales] > 100000 THEN "Large"
   ELSEIF [Sales] > 50000 THEN "Medium"
   ELSE "Small"
   END

3. Aggregated:
   SUM([Sales]) / COUNT(DISTINCT [Customer])
   = Average sales per customer

4. String:
   CONCAT([First Name], " ", [Last Name])
   UPPER([Product Name])
   SUBSTRING([Email], 0, FIND([Email], "@")-1)

5. Date:
   DATEDIFF('day', [Order Date], [Ship Date])
   YEAR([Order Date])

Where to Calculate:

Option 1: Tableau Calculated Field
✓ Easy visual formula editor
✓ Stored with workbook
✗ Re-calculated for every view
✗ Slower for complex formulas

Option 2: Data Source Level
✓ Shared across all workbooks
✓ Pre-calculated once
✗ Requires data source edit
✗ Less accessible

Option 3: Database (Best)
✓ Fastest (pre-calculated)
✓ Consistent definition
✓ Works across all tools
✗ Requires SQL access
```

### Aggregation Rules

```
Rule 1: Detail Level Determines Aggregation
[Region] in rows → [Sales] aggregated by region
[Region] + [Product] in rows → [Sales] by region-product combo

Rule 2: Measures Always Aggregated
Can't show raw value unless detail level = 1 record
[Customer Name] + [Order Date] = 1 row per order
  Can show [Order Amount] (one value per record)

[Customer Name] only = multiple orders per customer
  Must aggregate [Order Amount] (SUM, AVG, etc)

Rule 3: Aggregation Respects Filters
SUM([Sales]) with [Year]=2024 → Sum only 2024 sales
Different from total minus prior years (context matters)

Rule 4: LOD Controls Aggregation Scope
Normal: Aggregates to visible dimensions
FIXED: Aggregates to specific dimensions (ignores visible)
INCLUDE: Adds dimensions to aggregation
EXCLUDE: Removes dimensions from aggregation
```

### Data Types & Their Role

```
String (Text):
├─ Dimensions by default
├─ Use for: Categories, names, descriptions
└─ Aggregation: COUNT, COUNT(DISTINCT)

Number (Integer, Real):
├─ Measures by default
├─ Use for: Quantities, amounts, rates
└─ Aggregation: SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, MEDIAN

Date:
├─ Dimension (but special behavior)
├─ Creates hierarchy: Year → Month → Day
├─ Automatic formatting based on use
└─ Can be continuous (line charts) or discrete (bar charts)

Geographic:
├─ Special string with geocoding built-in
├─ Enables automatic map creation
├─ Fields: Country, State, City, Latitude, Longitude
└─ Tableau includes geocoding database
```

### Continuous vs Discrete (The Most Confusing Concept)

```
DISCRETE (Individual values):
- Tableau: Blue background
- Pill appearance: Distinct boxes
- Use: Categories, filtering
- Result: One bar per value (4 regions → 4 bars)

Example:
[Region] discrete: West, East, South, North = 4 bars

CONTINUOUS (Range of values):
- Tableau: Green background
- Pill appearance: Merged pill
- Use: Axes, color gradients
- Result: Gradient across range

Example:
[Sales] continuous: $0 to $1M = gradient scale

How Tableau Decides:

String fields:
- Default discrete (categories)

Number fields:
- Default continuous (can be summed)

Date fields:
- Used whole (year, month): Discrete
- Used at detail level (exact date): Continuous
- Used as continuous: Gradient scale from min to max date

When to Override:

Make discrete:
Right-click [Metric] pill → Discrete
Use when: Want separate bucket (2024, 2025, 2026)
Not: Want continuous gradient

Make continuous:
Right-click [Region] pill → Continuous
Use when: Want color gradient by region (unusual)
Not: Usually keep discrete
```

### Filters Deep Dive

```
Tableau Filter Types (Applied In Order):

1. EXTRACT FILTERS
- Applied BEFORE importing data
- "Only import year 2024"
- Fastest (less data imported)

2. DATA SOURCE FILTERS
- Applied to all worksheets
- Consistent across workbook
- "Always exclude cancelled orders"

3. CONTEXT FILTERS
- Applied BEFORE aggregation
- Filters raw data first
- Then dimensions aggregate
- Use when: Need to filter detail before aggregation

4. WORKSHEET FILTERS (Regular)
- Applied AFTER aggregation
- Most common type
- Filters aggregated results
- Interactive (user can change)

5. MEASURE FILTERS
- Filters by aggregated value
- "Show only regions with sales > $100k"
- Applied to results

6. TABLE CALCULATION FILTERS
- Filters computed values
- "Show top 10"
- Applied last

Filter Rules:

Multiple filters are AND (all must match):
[Region] = "West" AND [Year] = 2024

Optional filters can be OR:
[Category] IN ("A", "B", "C")

Null handling:
By default: Nulls included (shown)
Can exclude: Filter → None
```

### Performance Principles

```
The Tableau Query Pipeline:

Raw Data (Database)
    ↓
1. Extract filters / Data source filters (reduce rows early)
    ↓
2. Context filters (reduce rows before aggregation)
    ↓
3. Aggregation (group by dimensions)
    ↓
4. Regular filters (filter aggregated results)
    ↓
5. Table calculations (compute on filtered aggregated data)
    ↓
Display

Performance Sweet Spots:

✓ Fast:
- Row count < 100k (after context filters)
- Dimensions < 10
- Measures < 5 per worksheet
- LOD < 3 dimensions

⚠️ Medium:
- Row count 100k-1M
- Dimensions 10-20
- Complex calculations

❌ Slow:
- Row count > 1M (not filtered)
- Dimensions > 30
- Nested LOD expressions
- Calculations in LOD

Optimization Checklist:
□ Create extract with filters
□ Add context filter first
□ Reduce detail rows shown
□ Pre-aggregate in database
□ Hide unused sheets
□ Test on actual data volume
```

